    "chapterNumber": 8,
    "title": "The Marks on the Wall",
    "setting": "Years after the Shade — survivors underground",
    "wordCount": 1385,
    "paragraphs": [
      "Maya Strand does not sleep after the survey. She sits in her quarters in C-3 with the lamp turned low and looks at her hands, which are still marked with the chalk from the wall of the aperture chamber, two points, a hand's width apart, and she understands that she is carrying the most important thing anyone has carried in ten years, and she does not know how to put it down.",
      "At 0600 she goes to the council room. The council of eleven is already there, all of them, which means the word has travelled the way words travel in a place this small, through walls and vents and the silence between sentences. They look at her when she enters, and she sees in their faces that they already know something, though they do not know what.",
      "She sits at the head of the table. The table is a sheet of galvanised steel bolted to the floor, and the chairs are plastic, and the walls are concrete, and none of it has changed in ten years, and everything has changed in the last twelve hours.",
      "\"The second survey is complete,\" Maya says. \"Kade, Jin, Lia, and myself reached the aperture chamber at 0400. The light arrived at 0417. Duration: eleven seconds. Temperature rise: four degrees.\" She pauses. \"And the light was higher than yesterday. By the width of a hand.\"",
      "The room is silent. Eugene Pryce, who is seventy-one and remembers the before-times, who filed a map in May 2032 that he does not remember filing, leans forward. \"Higher?\"",
      "\"I marked the wall. Yesterday's mark is here. Today's light was here.\" Maya touches two points on the table, the way she touched them on the wall, and the council watches her do it, and the distance is small but the meaning is vast.",
      "\"The window is moving,\" Kade says. He has not spoken since they returned, nine hours of walking in silence, and his voice is rough with it.",
      "\"Or the source is moving,\" Jin says.",
      "\"Or someone is tracking us,\" Lia says.",
      "They look at her. Lia is seventeen and she has just spoken the thing that the others were thinking but did not say, and she does not blush or apologise because she has spent nine hours walking in the dark with three people who do not apologise for what they think.",
      "\"Tracking us how?\" Eugene asks.",
      "\"The aperture is quartz,\" Lia says. \"Quartz doesn't move. It's set in rock. If the light is hitting higher on the quartz, either the light is coming from a different angle, or the quartz has been adjusted.\"",
      "\"Adjusted by who?\"",
      "\"I don't know.\"",
      "Maya holds up her hand. The council falls silent. \"We have two facts,\" she says. \"Fact one: for ten years, light has arrived at this aperture twice a day at 0417. Fact two: today, that light was higher than yesterday. We do not know why. We do not know what it means. But we know that something has changed, and that change is the first change we have seen in ten years.\"",
      "She looks around the table. \"I propose that we announce this to the general bunker.\"",
      "\"Clause four,\" someone says. \"No announcement until—\"",
      "\"Clause four was my clause,\" Maya says. \"I wrote it. I am suspending it.\"",
      "The room is silent again. Kade looks at his daughter. Lia looks back at him, and she nods, barely, and he understands that she is telling him it is alright, and he does not know if it is alright but he trusts her, and he nods back.",
      "\"When?\" Eugene asks.",
      "\"Now,\" Maya says. \"This morning. Before the morning count. Before people wake up and spend another day thinking nothing will ever change.\"",
      "She stands up. She is sixty-one and she has not slept in twenty hours and her knees hurt and her hands are marked with chalk and she is the most powerful person in the world because she is the only person who knows what the marks mean, and she is about to give that power away.",
      "\"Kade,\" she says. \"You will tell them what you saw. Jin, you will show them the map. Lia, you will stand with your father. Eugene, you will sit with me, and you will tell them that you filed the map, and that you do not remember filing it, and that not remembering is alright, because none of us remember everything, and the things we remember are not always the things that matter.\"",
      "\"What will you tell them?\" Kade asks.",
      "\"I will tell them that the light moved,\" Maya says. \"And I will tell them that we do not know what it means, and that not knowing is not the same as not hoping. And I will tell them that the first expedition in ten years left yesterday with four people, and that four people came back, and that the only thing we lost was the certainty that nothing would ever change.\"",
      "She walks toward the door. The council follows her. They walk through the corridors of the bunker, past the doors with their painted numbers, past the hydroponics bays with their green light, past the reactor room with its low hum, and they do not speak because they do not need to speak, because they are walking toward something that is bigger than anything they have words for.",
      "At the assembly hall, Maya stands on the raised platform where the council meets, and she looks out at the faces of two hundred people, and she sees in their eyes the same thing she has seen every morning for ten years, which is the look of people who have learned not to hope, and she understands that what she is about to say is dangerous, because hope is dangerous, and she says it anyway.",
      "\"The light moved,\" she says.",
      "The room is silent.",
      "\"Yesterday, four of us walked to the aperture. We saw the light. And today, the light was higher than yesterday. By the width of a hand.\" She holds up her hand. \"This much. This is how much the world changed while you were sleeping.\"",
      "She pauses. She looks at Kade, who is standing with his daughter, and she sees that he is afraid, and she sees that Lia is not, and she understands that this is the difference between them, and that the difference is not age or experience but something simpler and harder: Lia has not yet learned that hope can hurt.",
      "\"We do not know what it means,\" Maya says. \"We do not know if it will happen again. We do not know if the light is moving toward us or away from us. We do not know if someone is keeping a window open, or if the window is opening itself. We know almost nothing.\"",
      "She pauses again. The room is still silent, but the silence is different now. It is the silence of people who are listening, really listening, for the first time in ten years.",
      "\"But we know this,\" Maya says. \"For ten years, we have been walking north in the dark, and for ten years, the dark has not answered. Yesterday, the dark answered. It answered with light. And today, the light moved. And tomorrow, we will walk north again, and we will keep walking north until we find out what the light is saying, and we will not stop walking until we know.\"",
      "She steps down from the platform. The room does not applaud. The room is too full of something else for applause. It is full of the sound of two hundred people breathing in unison, and the sound of hope, which is quieter than despair but lasts longer.",
      "Kade finds her in the corridor afterward. \"You shouldn't have said we'd keep walking,\" he says. \"We don't know if we can.\"",
      "\"We don't know if we can't,\" Maya says. \"That's the point.\"",
      "She walks away from him, toward her quarters, toward sleep, toward a dream she has not had in ten years, which is the dream of light, real light, the kind that warms your skin and makes you squint, and she does not know if she will ever have it again, but she knows, for the first time in ten years, that it is possible."
    ]
