      "The truck to Baikonur took nineteen hours, and Riko Tanaka spent eleven of them reading the same nine lines of a cargo manifest on a phone that was not hers.",
      "She had taken the phone from the glove box of the armoured truck, standard issue, no SIM, wiped clean. It had exactly one file on it: a photograph of a manifest, taken by someone standing too close to a screen. Nine lines visible. Section 44. Launch vehicle designation scrubbed. Mass at separation: 340 kilograms. Destination: Lagrange point one, deployment bus four. Keys provisioned to: [REDACTED].",
      "Three hundred and forty kilograms. The size of a large motorcycle. Small enough to bolt to the side of a deployment bus, light enough that nobody would question the mass budget.",
      "She thought about the Helios Array. Two hundred and forty mirrored facets on a folding spine. A control module that was the only way to fold it once it was open. Keys provisioned ninety days ago, not to Amara, not to Yuki.",
      "To someone at Baikonur.",
      "The steppe came up at dawn, flat and brown and endless, and then the pads, rust-red gantries standing in the dust like the ribs of something that had died standing up. Kwame Osei's checkpoint was a ring of container boxes painted green, and the men at the wire had rifles and they had discipline, which was more than Riko had expected.",
      "She gave them the name Chen Wei had given her. They made her wait four hours in a box with no window, and then a man with a limp walked her across the pad to a control bunker that had once been Soviet and was now something else.",
      "Kwame Osei was younger than his voice on the radio. He was fifty-two and he looked forty and he was eating dates from a tin with his fingers. He did not offer her one.",
      "\"Riko Tanaka,\" he said. \"Garrett Voss's head of site security. Relieved yesterday at noon. You have been driving for nineteen hours to ask me a question.\"",
      "\"You monitor Exodus traffic.\"",
      "\"I monitor everything. It is cheaper than fighting.\" He put the tin down. \"Ask.\"",
      "\"A module,\" Riko said. \"Three hundred and forty kilograms. Section 44. Going up on a deployment bus to L1. Keys provisioned ninety days ago to someone who is not the design team.\"",
      "Kwame was quiet for a long time. Outside, a generator changed pitch.",
      "\"You are asking me if I sold a launch slot to your employer,\" he said at last.",
      "\"I'm asking if you sold one to someone using my employer's keys.\"",
      "\"There is a difference?\"",
      "\"Yes.\"",
      "He looked at her for a while, and then he opened a drawer and took out a folder, paper, actual paper, and turned it around on the desk so she could read it.",
      "It was a launch services contract. The buyer's name was a shell she did not recognize, but the technical annex had an Exodus Solutions watermark, and the signature authority at the bottom was a name she did recognize: H. Castellanos, Garrett's head of orbital operations. The slot was pad 31, fourteen weeks out. Payload: one deployment bus, mass at separation 340 kg, destination L1.",
      "\"He is not sending the array up first,\" Riko said. \"He is sending the lid.\"",
      "\"He is sending the lid up before the pot,\" Kwame said. \"So that when the pot arrives, the lid is already waiting, and the keys are already in the lock, and the person who holds the keys decides whether the pot ever boils.\"",
      "\"Who has the keys?\"",
      "\"The module arrives here in nine days for integration. After that it is under my guard until launch. The keys go up with it, in a safe on the bus.\" He closed the folder. \"The person who provisioned them is in a tower six hundred kilometres from your mountain. The person who will use them is whoever he hands them to on the day the sky goes dark.\"",
      "Riko sat very still. Nineteen hours of steppe. Eleven years of standing guard over things she did not understand.",
      "\"The slot on pad 31,\" she said. \"Does it have a crew complement?\"",
      "\"It is uncrewed.\"",
      "\"Does it have a seat?\"",
      "Kwame smiled for the first time, and it was not a kind smile, but it was not unkind either. \"Everything has a seat, if you know how to weld. Are you asking for one?\"",
      "\"I'm asking what it costs.\"",
      "\"Forty kilograms of dry mass,\" he said. \"On the first ascent. Plus something I have been saving for the thing that goes last.\"",
      "\"What thing?\"",
      "\"I will tell you when it is last.\"",
      "She nodded. She did not have forty kilograms, but she knew a woman with thirty vehicles and a talent for finding mass that did not officially exist.",
      "\"Nine days,\" Riko said. \"The module is here in nine days.\"",
      "\"Nine days,\" Kwame agreed. \"After that, it is a problem for people with rockets.\"",
      "She stood up. He stood up too, and for a moment they looked at each other across the desk, two people who had both decided, in the same hour, to stop working for the men who were ending the world.",
      "\"You were relieved yesterday at noon,\" Kwame said. \"He will have your name on a watch list by tonight. You cannot go back to the mountain.\"",
      "\"I know.\"",
      "\"So what will you do?\"",
      "\"Go back to the mountain,\" Riko said, \"and steal the woman he thinks is still locked inside it.\""
