    "paragraphs": [
      "The morning count takes longer than it used to, because people stop Kira to ask about the light.",
      "She is eleven doors into Hallway C — fifteen doors, thirteen occupied, one of them hers — and already she has been stopped three times. Old Marchetti in C-4 wants to know if the light moving means the surface is safe. It does not mean that. The boy Tam in C-9 wants to know if his father was on the expedition. He was not. His father is dead six years. Kira tells him this gently, the way she has told him before, because Tam forgets, and forgetting is one of the things they are allowed to do down here.",
      "At C-11, Halloway's door, she stops. The door is still painted. Nobody has unpainted it. In the before-times, when someone died, you painted the door so the paint was new and the loss was marked, and after a while someone unpainted it, because grief has a shelf life like everything else. Nobody has unpainted Halloway's door. It has been weeks. The paint is no longer new, and the loss is no longer marked, and Kira stands in front of it with her clipboard and understands that the bunker has decided, without deciding, to leave this one door painted forever, as a place to put the thing they cannot name.",
      "She writes: C-11, unoccupied, door painted.",
      "She writes it every morning. She will write it every morning for the rest of her life, or until the paint peels.",
      "In the refectory, the mood is wrong. She has run the morning count for six years and she knows the refectory the way a sailor knows a harbour: by its silences. This morning the silence is full. People are talking in low voices at the tables, and when she walks in they do not stop talking, which is new. For ten years, the rule has been: do not talk about hope in the refectory, because hope is bad for digestion and worse for discipline.",
      "They are talking about hope.",
      "Eugene Pryce is sitting alone at the end of the long table, with his tray untouched, looking at the wall. Kira knows that look. It is the look of a man who is watching something that is not there. She sits down across from him.",
      "\"You were on the survey,\" she says.",
      "\"I was in the assembly.\" Eugene does not look at her. \"Maya said the light moved.\"",
      "\"Did it?\"",
      "\"She doesn't lie.\" He turns the mug in his hands. \"Kira. Do you know what I did, in the before-times?\"",
      "\"You were a clerk.\"",
      "\"I was a clerk in a records office. I filed things. Maps, mostly. Ordnance surveys. I filed them and I forgot them, because that is what a clerk does.\" He looks at her now, and his eyes are wet, and he is seventy-one years old and he is afraid. \"I filed a map in May 2032. Maya has it. It is in my hand. I do not remember filing it. I do not remember drawing it. And I keep thinking — what else did I file? What else did I draw, and forget, and what does it mean that a man can do a thing and not remember doing it?\"",
      "Kira does not know what to say. She is the morning-count keeper. She counts doors. She is not equipped for questions about what it means that a man can forget his own handwriting.",
      "\"Maybe you were busy,\" she says.",
      "\"Maybe.\" Eugene looks back at the wall. \"Or maybe the dark makes us forget things on purpose, so we don't have to carry them. And maybe the light — \" he stops.",
      "\"Maybe the light what?\"",
      "\"Maybe the light makes us remember.\" He stands up, leaves his tray, walks toward the corridor. \"If it moves again, Kira, someone should watch me. Someone should watch what I do next.\"",
      "She watches him go. She does not understand. She writes nothing down, because some things are not for the clipboard.",
      "At the end of the count, she does what she has not done in six years: she goes to the assembly hall, where two hundred people are gathered without being called, where Maya is standing at the back with her arms folded, where Kade and Lia are standing together near the door, where the air itself is different, warmer, as if the light that came through the quartz at 0417 did not entirely leave.",
      "Kira stands in the doorway and counts them, out of habit, two hundred, all present, and she understands that the count has changed its meaning. For ten years she counted survivors. This morning she is counting people who are waiting.",
      "Waiting is not surviving. Waiting is something else. Waiting is the thing before the door opens."
    ]
