      "They go back out before the council has finished arguing, because the light does not wait for votes.",
      "Yorke stands on the bow of Kael's boat with the compass in one hand and the notebook in the other, and behind her the city is already arguing — she can hear it across the water, the sound of five hundred people who have just been told the ground under them is a tide. Reika wanted to wait for the second post to be planned properly. Kael said the light was moving while they were planning. That was the whole meeting. Kael's boat left with four people aboard: Kael, Yorke, Emiko, and Nikos, who showed up at the pontoon with a thermos and said, \"My nets can wait. The dark cannot.\"",
      "They motor east for six hours. The water changes under them — Yorke feels it before she sees it, the grey creeping in at the edges of the gold, the way a bruise creeps in at the edges of a wound. She takes readings every fifteen minutes. Each one is worse than the last.",
      "\"Here,\" she says at noon.",
      "Kael cuts the motor. The silence that follows is the silence of deep water, which is not silence at all but a hum too low to hear, a hum you feel in your teeth.",
      "Emiko and Kael go over the side with the second post. Nikos stands at the rail with a gaff, watching the water the way his grandfather watched hillsides. Yorke takes bearings on the sun, such as it is — a pale disc behind the haze, dimmer here than it should be, dimmer than it was at the first post, dimmer than it was yesterday.",
      "The welding takes three hours. Yorke writes numbers. At 1430 she writes a number she does not want to write: the eastern edge intensity is now twenty-two percent of centre. Yesterday it was thirty. The day before, forty.",
      "Two posts. Two points. It is not enough for a curve, but it is enough for a line, and the line is going down faster than the last line, and she does the arithmetic three times because she wants it to be wrong, and it is not wrong.",
