    "wordCount": 861,
    "paragraphs": [
      "The corporate people came down from the tower with tablets and a list, and they set up in the commissary and called the names in alphabetical order, which told Amara they had learned nothing yet, because a leak does not keep its name in the alphabet.",
      "Her turn came on the second morning. The interviewer was a man named Doan, young, with the expensive stillness of somebody paid to be still. He had her file open on the tablet and did not look at it once.",
      "\"Your head engineer runs a relay node off the fabrication floor.\"",
      "\"She runs several. At my request. We mirror the design files so a power failure can't take a week's work.\"",
      "\"This node had a listener.\"",
      "She had known for two days. She had lain in her bunk in the communal quarters with it, counting rivets in the ceiling while the woman in the next bunk pretended to sleep, and the work of the next ten minutes was to look like a woman hearing it now.",
      "\"A listener,\" she said.",
      "\"Traffic copied off the mirror onto a leased line. Six hundred kilometres.\" Doan turned the tablet around. A map. A circle. The same circle Yuki had drawn, except this one had a flag on it, and the flag was the Exodus tower, and Doan was watching her face the way you watch a gauge. \"Billed to site security. Equipment rental. Ms Tanaka's budget.\"",
      "\"Riko signed the aggregate. I was there. Line items were her job.\"",
      "\"Her job is vacant.\"",
      "\"Then you've answered yourself. I can't help you with the line.\"",
      "\"Mr. Voss wants the fabrication floor sealed while the benches are swept. Three days.\"",
      "\"You can't seal my floor for three days. The spine is in jig. A paused layup is a failed layup. That is two hundred tonnes of graphene and a schedule you do not have.\"",
      "\"You have a deployment when Mr. Voss says you have a deployment.\" Doan closed the file. \"Thank you for your time, Doctor.\"",
      "She walked out of the commissary into the white afternoon and stood in the lee of the deposition hall and understood the shape of the new world. The array was not hers. It had not been hers for some time. She was only now being told.",
      "The floor was sealed at 1800. The technicians came out with their badges on lanyards and their hands in their pockets, forty of them, standing in the dust while men they did not know walked the benches with wands and cameras. The chalk truss ran out the loading door and ended abruptly at the seal-line tape, as if the building itself had been cut off mid-sentence.",
      "She found Yuki after midnight at the water tower, where the smokers used to go before smoking was confiscated. Yuki was sitting on the curb with her coat over her shoulders, watching the lights of the security trucks move along the ridge.",
      "\"They'll reconstruct the mirror,\" Amara said. \"When they do, they'll find the push. Not the listener. The other thing. What you sent.\"",
      "\"I know.\"",
      "\"Riko said Osaka.\"",
      "\"Riko is on a watch list, and by tomorrow night so am I. There is no Osaka, Amara. There was never going to be an Osaka. Riko says things like that so the parting costs her something.\" Yuki pulled the coat tighter. \"She'll be back tomorrow. Don't wait up for her either.\"",
      "They sat a while. Down in the hall, behind the sealed doors, the deposition rig ticked as it cooled, the way it ticked every night, the sound of a thing that does not know it has been stopped.",
      "\"Can I ask you something technical?\" Yuki said.",
      "\"Ask.\"",
      "\"The array deploys on a burn command from the ground. Once the spine starts to unfold, the sequence is autonomous — two hundred and forty facets, four-tenths of a degree apart, seventy hours to full span. What happens if the command is sent before the control module docks?\"",
      "Amara felt the night go very quiet.",
      "\"The module is the only way to fold it once it's open,\" she said. \"Without it the coverage locks wherever it locks. Ninety-nine point four percent, and no hand on the fold, forever. You know that. Why are you asking me something you already know?\"",
      "Yuki looked at her. The lamp on the tower was behind her and her face was in shadow and Amara could not read it, and then she could, and the reading of it was the worst thing that had ever happened to her — worse than the asteroid, worse than the deadline, because the asteroid had never once been her decision.",
      "\"Yuki. Whatever you are thinking—\"",
      "\"I am thinking you taught me to solve impossible problems,\" Yuki said, and stood, and walked back toward the barracks with her coat over her shoulders. Amara sat on the curb for a long time after, under the water tower, in the smell of other people's confiscated cigarettes, and did not go after her, because going after her would mean deciding, and she was not ready to decide, and not deciding was a decision, and they both knew it."
    ]
