    "paragraphs": [
      "The light moves every morning now.",
      "Not every morning by much. A hand's width the first day. A finger's width the day after. Then a hand again, then two hands, so that the marks on the aperture chamber wall have become a small crowd of chalk points climbing the stone, and Maya has started taking a different witness to every 0417, because a thing seen by one person is a story and a thing seen by eleven is a fact.",
      "They queue to be the witness. That is new. For ten years the aperture was a rumour at the end of a nine-hour walk, and now there is a rota, and people trade duties to be on it, and the ones who go come back down the corridor with a look the others have started to call, not kindly, the sunstruck look.",
      "The bunker rearranges itself. It does not do it by vote. It does it the way water rearranges itself when the ground tilts — slowly, and then all at once. The people who speak of walking north begin to move their blankets to the rooms at the far end of Hallway C, to be near the others who speak of it. The people who do not speak of it drift toward the core, toward the reactor hum, toward Amara Voss and her logged certainties. Nobody announces the moves. Kira notices because noticing is her job.",
      "Fifteen doors. Thirteen occupied. The count does not change. The names behind the doors change, and the count does not change, and Kira stands in the corridor with her clipboard and understands that she is watching the bunker divide itself into two countries, and that the border between them is not a wall but a question, and the question is: what do you do with a light that moves?",
      "In the refectory, Marcus says what the walkers are thinking. He has seventeen sorties under the protocol, more than anyone alive, and he stands up with his tray in his hands and says, \"Four hours and two kilometres. That was the leash. I am not saying the leash was wrong. I am saying the world that needed the leash is dead, and we are still wearing it.\"",
      "\"And when your core temperature drops?\" someone says.",
      "\"Then it drops walking toward something.\" He sits down. He does not eat. Nobody eats while Marcus is standing and nobody starts again when he sits, because he has said the thing and now the thing is in the room with them, eating their food.",
      "In the hydroponics bay, Kade watches his daughter prune tomatoes and does not know when she learned to do it so well. The LED arrays hum. The water cycles. Lia's hands are sure among the vines.",
      "\"The estimate is four thousand kilometres,\" he says. \"You heard your father's scout say it. Four thousand. Nobody walks four thousand kilometres in the dark. Nobody walks four hundred.\"",
      "\"The light is coming closer,\" Lia says. \"Every day. You taught me to read the marks.\"",
      "\"It is not coming closer. It is pointing higher. We don't know what it means.\"",
      "\"It means someone is adjusting the window.\" She does not look up from the vines. \"Somebody has kept a window open for us for ten years, Papa. Twice a day, every day. And now they are adjusting it, and you want to argue about what it means while they are waiting for an answer.\"",
      "\"What answer could we possibly give?\"",
      "\"Ourselves,\" Lia says, and cuts a stem clean through, and lays it in the basket like a sentence finished.",
      "Maya finds Amara Voss in the reactor gallery at the end of third shift, logging output the way she has logged output every day for ten years, the same handwriting, the same columns. Maya watches her finish the line before she speaks.",
      "\"If people walk,\" Maya says, \"what does the core need?\"",
      "Amara does not ask what she means. That is how Maya knows the question has already occurred to her, and been answered, and filed.",
      "\"Sixty,\" Amara says. \"I can run this core with sixty hands and hold safety margins. We are two hundred. The bunker can spare more than it can spare, if it comes to that.\" She closes the log. \"I am not recommending it, Maya. I am logging it. There is a difference.\"",
      "\"There used to be,\" Maya says.",
      "At morning count, Kira walks Hallway C with her clipboard and writes the numbers that do not change. Fifteen doors. Thirteen occupied. At C-11 she stops, as she stops every morning, and there is something new on Halloway's painted door: a small chalk arrow, drawn at eye height, pointing down the corridor. Pointing north.",
      "She stands and looks at it for a long time. Then she does a thing she has never done in six years of counts. She turns her clipboard over, and on the back, where nobody is supposed to write, she writes it down anyway: arrangement changed.",
      "The arrow is still there at the evening count. It is still there the next morning. Nobody rubs it out. After a while it is simply part of the door, the way the paint is part of the door, the way the dead are part of the living, and the corridor learns to walk past it without looking, the way you learn not to look at the sun."
    ]
