      "They found it on the third day, the way Amara had known they would, and they did not even have the decency to find it quietly.",
      "Doan came to the commissary with two security men and stood at the door until the room went silent, and then he read the name off his tablet like a man calling a flight.",
      "\"Sato, Yuki.\"",
      "Yuki stood up from her tray. She had been eating, or moving food around a plate. She set the fork down and aligned it with the plate's edge, which was so completely Yuki that Amara had to look away.",
      "The reconstruction had found the push. Not the listener — the listener belonged to someone in Garrett's own tower, and that investigation had gone nowhere with impressive speed. The push. The fourteen pages. The routing was buried under three relays and a dead drop, and Doan's people had dug it out in fifty-one hours because they were very good, because they were paid to be very good, and because Yuki had built the route to be found eventually, the way you build a fuse to burn.",
      "Amara stood up.",
      "\"She's my head engineer. The floor reopens in four hours. If you take her, the spine fails, and when the spine fails you can explain to Mr. Voss why the deployment window moved.\"",
      "\"The floor doesn't reopen until I say so, Doctor.\"",
      "\"Then call him. Call Mr. Voss and tell him you have the leaker and the array in the same room and you can only keep one.\" Amara heard her own voice come out level and did not recognize it. \"I'll wait.\"",
      "Doan looked at her for a long moment. Then he took Yuki Sato out of the commissary between the two security men, and the door swung shut, and the room stayed silent, and Amara sat back down and picked up her coffee with a hand that did not shake until the door stopped moving.",
      "She found Garrett on the pad at dusk, standing by the helicopter, talking to the pilot about weather. She waited until he finished. He watched her wait the way he watched everything, as if it were a line item.",
      "\"You found your leak,\" she said.",
      "\"We found a push.\" Garrett zipped his jacket against the evening wind. \"Fourteen pages, routed through three relays. Very elegant. She nearly got away with it.\"",
      "\"Nearly?\"",
      "\"The route was built to be found, Amara. Eventually. That's not a mistake. That's a message.\" He looked at her. \"You knew. That's why you're here instead of in your bunk.\"",
      "\"I know that if she disappears into a corporate hole, the array launches crippled or doesn't launch. You have maybe six people alive who can fly that spine. You've just arrested the best one.\"",
      "\"You want her back on the floor.\"",
      "\"I want her on the floor under guard, on camera, with a minder at the bench and a dead-man switch on her terminal. You can watch her blink. But she finishes the spine.\" Amara kept her hands in her pockets so he could not see them. \"Or you can put her in a hole and weld the array yourself.\"",
      "Garrett was quiet for a while. Out beyond the wire, the desert was going the colour of old ash.",
      "\"You've changed,\" he said. \"Six months ago you would have handed her over and asked for a new engineer.\"",
      "\"Six months ago I thought I was building a lifeboat.\"",
      "\"You are building a lifeboat.\"",
      "\"I am building something with a lid, Garrett. You said so yourself, in a helicopter, in front of my security chief. So let's not pretend we're still the people who shook hands on eighteen months.\"",
      "He laughed, once, without humour. Then he keyed his radio.",
      "\"Doan. The engineer goes back to the floor. Full surveillance, terminal air-gapped, escorted everywhere including the latrine. If she so much as draws a diagram on a napkin, I want the napkin.\" He let go of the key. \"You have your spine surgeon, Doctor. Four months. And Amara — \" he turned, and for the first time since the caldera she saw the man underneath the schedule, and the man was afraid, and the fear was old, \" — whatever she was going to do with those fourteen pages, she'd have done it by now if she could. Whatever she's waiting for hasn't arrived yet. When it arrives, I want to know what it is before she does.\"",
      "He got into the helicopter and was gone into the darkening east, and Amara stood on the pad and thought: he's right. She's waiting for something. The module. The keys. The dock.",
      "She's waiting for the lid to be within reach, so she can slam it.",
      "And the thought that came next arrived quietly, like a measurement settling: then I had better decide before it gets here.",
      "She walked back toward the sealed hall, where the chalk truss ran out under the tape into the dark, whitening, waiting, drawn full size so that someone, someday, could build it."
