    "paragraphs": [
      "Riko came back on the sixth day, after Amara had stopped watching the road.",
      "She came through the wire in a fuel bowser with Kyrgyz plates and a forged manifest, and she was sitting in the fabrication hall when Amara came in from the night shift, drinking tea from a stolen mug like she had never left.",
      "\"You're on a watch list,\" Amara said.",
      "\"So is the bowser. We watch each other.\" Riko set the mug down. \"I saw Kwame.\"",
      "She told it all. The contract. The shell buyer. H. Castellanos's signature on the technical annex. Three hundred and forty kilograms, a deployment bus, pad 31, fourteen weeks. Keys in a safe on the bus, going up with the module, to be handed on the day to whoever Garrett had chosen to hold the lid.",
      "\"There's a seat,\" Riko said. \"Uncrewed bus, but Kwame can cut a seat. Price is forty kilograms of dry mass on the first ascent, plus something he won't name yet. He calls it the thing that goes last.\"",
      "\"Forty kilograms of what?\"",
      "\"Doesn't matter. Mass is mass. He wants leverage against Exodus and he wants it denominated in something he can weigh.\" Riko leaned forward. \"That's not the problem. The problem is the seat is one seat. The keys go up on the bus. Somebody has to ride it, dock with the module before the deploy window, and either take the keys or break them. And the somebody has to be able to fly a deployment bus, dock on manual, and do math under fire.\"",
      "\"You,\" Amara said.",
      "\"Me. I'm the best pilot either of us knows. But Amara — \" Riko's voice did something it had not done before, a small thing, a catch, \" — if I go up, I don't come down. There's no return vehicle on an uncrewed bus. The bus docks with the array or it doesn't, and either way it isn't coming home. Kwame was clear. It's a one-way ticket. I'm not afraid of it. I'm telling you because the plan has to know.\"",
      "They sat with it. Down the hall, the night crew was welding a rib, and the light of it came under the door in pulses, like a heartbeat.",
      "\"There's a second thing,\" Riko said. \"Kwame's man walked me out past the refugee staging. There's a woman running the convoys. His operations people call her the quartermaster. I knew her face before anyone said her name. She's Garrett's daughter, Amara. The estranged one. She's been on the ground with Kwame for two years, moving refugees for food and passage, and Garrett either doesn't know or pretends he doesn't. If he ever wants to get off this rock, the only road to a pad runs through the man his daughter works for. That's Kwame's leverage. Not the forty kilos. Her.\"",
      "Amara thought about Garrett on the pad at dusk, afraid under the schedule. A man with a tower and a company and one child on the wrong side of the world.",
      "\"Don't use her,\" Amara said.",
      "\"I didn't say use. I said she's the road.\" Riko finished the tea. \"Use is a choice. The road exists either way.\"",
      "A runner from the security office found them there at 0300. The module had landed at Baikonur.",
      "It had come in ahead of the contract date — twelve days early, no notice, an Exodus heavy-lifter that requested landing clearance forty minutes out and gave Kwame's controllers no time to do anything but grant it. It was on the ground now, in a hangar Kwame did not control, guarded by people Castellanos had sent with it, and the integration schedule had moved from nine days to immediate.",
      "\"He knew,\" Riko said, standing. \"Castellanos. Or Garrett. They moved it early because the leak made them nervous. The nine-day window was never nine days. It was until they got scared.\"",
      "\"What do we have?\"",
      "\"The bus rolls to pad 31 in six days. Keys go aboard at roll-out.\" Riko was already moving, already calculating, the pilot's arithmetic running under her voice. \"The seat has to be cut before roll-out, and the mass has to be on the manifest, and I have to be at Baikonur before the pad closes. I have maybe five days, Amara. And I need forty kilograms of something worth more to Kwame than money.\"",
      "\"Zara,\" Amara said. \"She owes me. And she has everything that's worth anything.\"",
      "\"Zara it is.\" Riko stopped at the door. The welding light pulsed under it. \"Six days to the pad, fourteen weeks to the window, one seat, no way home. When it's time, you'll know, because the sky will tell you.\" She looked back. \"Yuki knows what she's doing. Make sure she's on the console when the window opens. Whatever she does next, she has to be at the console to do it.\"",
      "Then she was gone into the dark between the buildings, and Amara stood alone in the hall with the chalk truss running out the door into the night, full size, waiting, and understood that the countdown had not started with the rocket. It had started the day Garrett said four months. They had simply not been able to hear it yet."
    ]
