    "wordCount": 1091,
    "paragraphs": [
      "The walkers do not get to simply walk. That is the first thing Kade learns as the de facto quartermaster of a departure he will not join: a hundred and thirty-one people cannot go north into the dark on hope alone, because hope is not a ration.",
      "He builds the list with Jin and Marcus in the old planning room, the one with the whiteboard nobody has erased in six years, because the last thing written on it was a duty roster from the before and nobody has had the heart. He erases it now. The marker is dry. He wets it with his thumb and it comes back, faintly, and he writes over the ghost: THE FINAL EXPEDITION.",
      "\"The corridor goes nine hours to the aperture,\" Jin says. \"After the aperture, the map is Eugene's. Filed 2032. A northern grid, a corridor that keeps climbing. We think it comes out near the surface. We think.\" He taps the laminate. \"Four thousand kilometres to the source. That's not one expedition. That's fifty. That's a relay.\"",
      "\"So it's not one expedition,\" Marcus says. \"It's a road. We build the road the way the walkers before us built roads. You walk a day, you cache, you walk back. You walk two days, you cache, you walk back. You carry the light with you as far as you can carry it, and you leave a lamp for the ones behind.\"",
      "They plan it for nine days.",
      "They do not call them caches, because a cache is for a road you intend to come back down, and nobody says out loud whether anyone is coming back. They call them waypoints. Waypoint One at the aperture. Waypoint Two at the end of the known corridor, nine hours on. Waypoint Three a full day past that, at the limit of a carried charge. Beyond Three, the map is a man's forgotten handwriting and a moving light, and the planning becomes faith, and faith, Kade has learned, is the thing you build the road out of when the materials run out.",
      "Amara Voss comes to the planning room on the fourth night.",
      "She stands in the doorway in her reactor-greys and watches the map take shape, the waypoints going up like a string of small lights on the whiteboard, and Kade sees her reading it the way an engineer reads a bridge plan — not for where it goes but for where it will fail.",
      "\"Waypoint Three is your breaking point,\" she says. \"A day's walk past the end of the known corridor, in the dark, at forty below. You can't carry enough charge to get a hundred and thirty-one people past Three. You'll get them to Three and you'll have to choose who goes on and who comes back, and the choosing will break them worse than the cold.\"",
      "\"You have a better plan,\" Kade says.",
      "\"I have a reactor.\" She steps into the room. \"The core runs on sixty hands. I've written my name under STAY, and the sixty-nine will keep this bunker lit for as long as there is fuel to split. But the bunker is not the only light we make.\" She puts a small case on the table. Inside, in foam, are ten lamps — not headlamps, not torches, but heavy, ugly, industrial lamps, reactor-service lamps, the kind that burn for a hundred days on a cell the size of a brick. \"The maintenance bay has been hoarding these for a decade against an emergency that never came. This is the emergency. Ten lamps. One per waypoint, and five for the dark past Three. A hundred days of light each. Carry them. Light the road. Let the people behind you see the people ahead.\"",
      "Kade looks at the lamps. He thinks about the chalk arrow on Halloway's door, and Amara's sentence under the columns — *the door stays open from both sides* — and he understands that she is not staying behind at all. She is doing the thing the keepers of lights have always done. She is holding the near end so the far end has somewhere to be from.",
      "\"You can't carry us,\" she says. \"So don't. Carry the light. We'll keep the door. That's the deal. That's the whole deal.\"",
      "The night before the departure, Lia finds her father in C-8, mending the strap on her pack that does not need mending.",
      "\"I keep trying to say the true thing early,\" she says. \"The way the refectory does. And I can't find it. There's no sentence big enough.\"",
      "\"There isn't one,\" Kade says. \"That's the thing nobody tells you. The sentence doesn't exist. People spend their whole lives looking for the sentence, and it doesn't exist, and that's not a failure. The love isn't in the sentence. The love is in the looking.\" He ties off the strap, tests it, hands her the pack. \"Walk north. See the sky. Come back and tell me about it, or don't come back and I'll know you're standing in it. Either way, I know where you are. You're my daughter. You're findable.\"",
      "At 0400 the walkers muster in the corridor.",
      "One hundred and thirty-one people in expedition suits, with sleds, with Amara's ten lamps distributed among the strongest, with Eugene's laminated map carried by Jin and a copy carried by Marcus, with three days' rations and the unspoken knowledge that three days is a beginning and not a plan.",
      "Maya sees them off. She is sixty-one and she is not walking — her name is under STAY, small and final — and she walks the line of them the way a general walks a line, except she is not inspecting them, she is memorizing them.",
      "\"The light moved,\" she says, when the line is quiet. \"That's all we know. The light moved, and for ten years it did not, and the difference between those two things is everything. You are the everything. Go and find out what's at the end of the adjustment, and if it's a person holding a window open, you tell them the people in the dark say thank you, and we're coming.\"",
      "Kade stands at the airlock with the sixty-nine behind him, and the outer door cycles, and the cold comes in like a living thing, and the walkers go through it in single file into the dark, and the last thing Kade sees before the door seals is his daughter's lamp, one of the ten, swinging on the back of Marcus's sled, going north, going away, going on."
    ]
