    "title": "The Scavenger's Price",
    "setting": "Near future — Earth races to escape",
    "wordCount": 866,
    "paragraphs": [
      "Zara Volkov's depot was an hour up the canyon in a place that had been a ski resort when there was snow, and Amara drove it alone because a convoy would be seen and she could no longer afford to be seen.",
      "The lodge was lit. Zara's people moved crates in the cold with the unhurried discipline of a professional army, thirty vehicles parked in lines by the lifts. Zara herself was on the terrace with a catalogue and a pencil, doing inventory by the light of a string of bulbs, and she did not look up when Amara came out of the dark.",
      "\"Forty technicians welding your spine,\" Zara said. \"Sixty-one tonnes of my graphene on your floor. And the lead engineer of the Helios Array drives an hour alone at night to see a woman who sells things. You need something heavy, Doctor.\"",
      "\"Forty kilograms of dry mass. On a manifest, at Baikonur, in five days.\"",
      "Now Zara looked up. The pencil stopped.",
      "\"Not graphene,\" she said. \"Graphene is on the Exodus manifest already and they'd count it. You need forty kilograms that Kwame Osei will take as payment for a seat and that Exodus security will not flag as forty kilograms of theft.\" She set the catalogue down. \"That's not mass, Amara. That's a favour. And a favour at Baikonur, in five days, with a warlord — that is the most expensive thing in the world. What are you buying with it?\"",
      "\"A seat on the deployment bus.\"",
      "\"For whom?\"",
      "\"It doesn't matter.\"",
      "\"It matters to the price.\" Zara came to the rail. In the light she looked every year of fifty, and none of it soft. \"I will tell you what I told Kwame when we were young and still partners: mass is honest, people are not. I can give you forty kilograms of honest mass — medical isotopes, seed stock, reactor-grade isotope heaters, things a cold world will pay anything for. Kwame will take it because he can weigh it and use it. But if the seat is for someone you love, the price is not the forty kilograms. The price is that I know who, so that when the sky does what your array is going to do, I know which name to put on the list of people I keep alive.\"",
      "They stood in the cold. The bulbs swung. Below, the crews went on loading, and Amara understood that Zara had known about the sky longer than anyone, that sixty-one tonnes of graphene was not a sale, it was a bet, and that the woman had been hedging against the Shade since before the Shade had a name.",
      "\"Riko,\" Amara said.",
      "Zara closed her eyes. She did not look surprised. She looked like a woman who had already paid a price once tonight and was now being asked to pay it again.",
      "\"The pilot,\" she said. \"Of course the pilot. It's always the ones who can fly who volunteer to fall.\" She was quiet for a moment. \"The seat is one-way.\"",
      "\"Yes.\"",
      "\"Then the forty kilograms is easy. It's the cheapest part of your night.\" Zara picked the pencil back up and wrote something in the margin of the catalogue, tore the page out, and handed it over. A manifest. Forty kilograms, itemized, forged with the fluency of a lifetime. \"I'll have it at Baikonur in four days, manifested as reactor-isotope heater stock, clean enough to pass an Exodus sniff. Kwame will take it because he knows I don't forge badly.\"",
      "\"What's the price?\" Amara asked.",
      "\"You've paid it. You told me the name.\" Zara looked at her for a long time, and something moved behind the inventory in her face. \"But I'll add one thing, because I'm a trader and I can't help it. When the lid comes down — and it will come down, whether your pilot reaches the keys or not, because too many people want it down for one woman in a bus to stop it alone — there will be a gap. There is always a gap. My network is already built for the dark, Amara. I've spent two years turning a salvage empire into a shadow-survival infrastructure, because I knew this was coming. When the gap opens, the people on the ground will need someone to tell them where the light is kept.\"",
      "\"Why are you telling me this?\"",
      "\"Because you're the one who's going to have to tell them.\" Zara turned back to the catalogue, and the interview was over. \"Go back to your mountain. Keep your saboteur at her console. And when the sky closes, Doctor — look for my lamps. I'll be the one keeping them lit.\"",
      "Amara drove down the canyon as the false dawn came up, the forged manifest on the seat beside her, forty kilograms of honest mass and a trader's prophecy, and she understood that the resistance she had imagined as a small and secret thing was neither small nor secret. It was everyone. It was the scavenger and the warlord and the mercenary and the engineer, all of them holding a different piece of the same door, waiting for the moment to pull it open."
    ]
