      "Isaiah Cross had been saying *shade* for eleven weeks, and on the day before the deploy window he stopped saying it, because something landed on his frequency that was worth more than a word.",
      "It was a file. Not fourteen pages this time. Forty. The full Helios deployment architecture, the Section 44 control module schematic, the key provisioning schedule, and — the thing that made Isaiah sit down hard in his broadcasting chair — the launch services contract from Baikonur, the shell buyer, the Castellanos signature, the whole spine of the lid, laid out in order, with annotations in a hand Isaiah did not know but would come to.",
      "He knew who had to have sent it. There were only three people alive who could have assembled it: a hacker in a shipping container, a mercenary on her way to a rocket, or an engineer who had decided to burn down her own life's work. He suspected it was all three, arriving on his frequency at once, the way a chord is three notes arriving at once.",
      "He put it on every frequency he had.",
      "\"*This is Isaiah Cross,*\" he said, and for the first time in a career of selling the end of the world, he was not selling anything. \"*I'm going to read you the instruction manual for the end of the world, because it turns out the end of the world has a lid, and the lid has keys, and the keys are going up on a rocket in—*\" he checked the contract, \"*—thirty-six hours. And here's the thing about lids and keys, people. A lid is only a prison if somebody's holding the keys. So the question isn't whether the sky goes dark. The question is whether anybody gets to decide where the dark falls.*\"",
      "He read the whole file. It took six hours. He read the coverage estimate — ninety-nine point four percent — and he read the module spec, and he read the key schedule, and he read the contract, and at the end he said the thing that would be quoted for however long there was anyone left to quote it:",
      "\"*Ninety-nine point four percent. That's the whole world. Which means the lid they're launching isn't a lid on the sky. It's a lid on everyone. And there's a woman on a bus and a woman at a console who are, right now, the only two people standing between the lid and the lock. I don't know their names. But I know what they're doing. They're trying to make sure that when the dark comes, it comes for everyone the same. Not for the people who paid. For everyone. That's the whole fight, people. That's the only fight there ever was.*\"",
      "Garrett Voss heard it in the tower, six hundred kilometres from the caldera, standing in front of a wall of monitors that had been showing him the deploy countdown.",
      "He stood very still for a long time.",
      "\"*Trace it,*\" he said.",
      "His ops chief shook his head. \"It's bounced off every relay from Lagos to Baikonur. And sir—\" the man swallowed, \"*—it's not just the broadcast. The file is out. Everywhere. FreeLight has it. Kwame has it. Every pirate frequency on the planet has it. The keys' schedule is out, sir. The whole plan is out.*\"",
      "Garrett looked at the countdown. Seventy-two hours. His array. His lid. His daughter somewhere on the wrong side of the world, and the only road to her running through a warlord who now knew exactly what the rocket on pad 31 was carrying.",
      "\"*Move it up,*\" he said. \"*Deploy at first window. I don't care about the alignment. I want that array off the ground before the ground reaches up and takes it.*\"",
      "\"Sir, the alignment—\"",
      "\"*I said move it up.*\"",
      "He turned from the monitors. He was afraid. He had been afraid for weeks, and the fear had a name now, and the name was that the lid was going to come down whether he willed it or not, and the only question left was whether he would be holding the keys when it did, and he was beginning — for the first time in a life of holding things — to understand that he would not.",
      "He picked up the phone and called Baikonur, to ask about a seat on the only other rocket that could still reach the array, the crewed extraction vehicle, the one he had been saving for himself. The road to it ran through Kwame Osei. The road to Kwame Osei ran through his daughter. He had not spoken to his daughter in two years.",
      "He made the call anyway."
